[Music]  sales of the previously high-flying  Boeing 737 are slumping amid the crisis  surrounding the newest version the max  Boeing the series booked only 36 737  orders and none in the past three months  last year at the mid-year point the  company had secured 296 orders for its  best-selling plane Boeing did get a  boost in June when British Airways  parent IAE said it would buy 200 max  planes though the deal still needs to be  finalized on top of the sales slump  boeing has more than 100 new max jets  sitting undelivered in its facilities  near seattle because the fleet has been  grounded since March more than 300  additional max planes already with  Airlines have also been idled by the  global grounding that's caused huge  disruptions across the aviation  landscape the crisis began last October  when a new max jet crashed on a flight  out of Indonesia it was the first crash  of a max less than five months later  another 737 max crash in Ethiopia a  total of 346 passengers and crew died  aboard those two flights the  similarities between the crashes sparked  safety concerns among regulators and  prompted a global grounding of the max  fleet the impact has reached far beyond  the countries where the planes went down  or Boeing's manufacturing hub in Seattle  for example take Southwest Airlines the  biggest buyer of max planes for now  the airline has struck the model from  flight schedules through October first  the budget airline had received 31 max  planes at the time the fleet was  grounded more were due for delivery over  the summer in response Southwest has had  to cancel flights and juggle schedules  United American and Air Canada are among  the other North American carriers to be  hit American has also had to cancel more  than 100 flights per day because of the  max grounding Air Canada has rented  planes from others including Lufthansa  and Qatar Airways to make up for the  shortage of seats with a max idled and  Iceland air has now struck the plane  from its schedule through October  passengers who are scheduled to fly  aboard Boeing's max fleet also remain  Radl  aiexander de Zuni ACK the  director-general of the world's biggest  airline Association said as much in  early June but the consequences of these  strategy go far beyond the technical  trust in the certification system has  been damaged among regulators between  regulators and the industry and with the  flying public other airlines around the  world that also rely on the Mac's are  scrambling as well many operators told  Boeing they expect to be compensated for  losses from the grounding consider the  case of European travel company tui one  of Europe's biggest max operators it  estimates an earnings hit of more than  330 million dollars this year alone that  bill covers the need to cancel some  flights and the need to rent replacement  aircraft at high cost to get customers  to their vacation hot spots those  involved in making the max also are  being impacted Boeing in April said it  would keep building 737s though at a  slower pace rather and cranking out 50  to 737s a month the company announced it  would build only 42 a month Boeing's  vast max supply chain also is grappling  with the turmoil Spirit AeroSystems said  some of its employees have been put on  shorter work weeks reducing their pay  investors haven't been immune either  after delivering a record number of  planes in 2018 bond shares reached a  record high early this year but since  then the aerospace Giants stock has  slumped also seeing their investments  fall our Spirit AeroSystems shareholders  the stock has declined about 25 percent  since March  earlier this year Boeing was hoping to  get the max fixed quickly but new  concerns with the plane systems have led  to delays for example in June an FAA  pilot found a new potential problem with  the 737 Max's flight control system  Boeing now says it won't submit its max  fix to the FA until September the FAA  and other regulators have not said when  they in turn may clear the plane to  resume carrying passengers until that  happens Boeing and its partners will  have to deal with the massive  all left by the beleaguered 737 max  [Music]

